// DD2 — the drag states: a drag that is in flight, the element it is over, and the drop.
//
// container: the drag is posted from outside the process by `xdotool` through XTEST — a press, a
// move, and a release on the X server the window really sits on. The Mac has no such server, and
// on this Mac a run *is* a window, which `scripts/window-guard.sh` forbids, so `scripts/probes.sh`
// and `scripts/tiers.sh` skip this one by name. It runs in the container:
//
//   ./scripts/gosbox.sh tiers tests/gui/dd2_drag_states.gos
//
// # What this exists for
//
// `DD2` is "drag threshold, drag-over/move states and drop-target feedback". The reason it was
// held open is worth writing down, because it is wrong.
//
// The register said: "`InteractiveElement::on_drag_move` (`elements/div.rs:282`) is a method on
// `Interactivity`, gpui's internal per-element state, so the binding cannot register one through
// the element vocabulary it builds." It can. `Interactivity` is a `pub` struct
// (`elements/div.rs:1478`), `InteractiveElement` is a `pub` trait whose drag methods are public
// (`:818`, `:976`, `:1132`), and the one thing in the way — `mod elements` being private
// (`gpui.rs:21`) — is undone by the line beneath it, `pub use elements::*` (`:77`). So
// `gpui::Interactivity` is a nameable path from another crate.
//
// What *was* true is that the binding could not make one **fire**. `on_drag_move`'s listener opens
// with `phase == DispatchPhase::Capture && let Some(drag) = &cx.active_drag` and a `TypeId` match
// (`:282-296`), and `cx.active_drag` is `pub(crate)` (`app.rs:545`) with two writers in the whole
// crate: the platform's file-drop translation, and the pending-mouse-down listener that arms a
// drag — which arms only when `drag_listener.take()` is `Some` (`:2158-2174`), and only `on_drag`
// ever puts one there (`:499`). So the arming half is not optional.
//
// # What the binding grew
//
// Three words on `gpui::on_mouse`, over gpui's own protocol rather than a state machine beside it:
//
//   * `drag` — this element is a **source**. gpui arms a real drag on it once the pointer has
//     moved more than `DRAG_THRESHOLD` (`2.0`, `elements/div.rs:47`) with the button down, carries
//     an `Arc<dyn Any>` payload in `cx.active_drag`, and draws a `DragPreview` — the element's own
//     subtree, copied out of the frame that described it — under the pointer.
//   * `over` — this element is a **target**. `on_drag_move` reports every move of a drag in flight
//     wherever it is, inside the element or outside it, and the binding compares
//     `DragMoveEvent::bounds` against `DragMoveEvent::event.position` itself, because gpui does
//     **not** gate this listener on hover the way it gates a press. The comparison is a
//     transition, so `<id>.over` is the state and `"<id>|over"` / `"<id>|out"` are the two
//     transitions that change it.
//   * `drop` — this element is a **target**. `on_drop` is dispatched on the release, on the bubble
//     phase, and only to an element the pointer is over (`:2094-2110`), which is the whole of what
//     "drop-target feedback" means.
//
// The payload is the source element's own id — a `String` the program chose — and it reaches the
// target as `<id>.drag_value` on over, out and drop alike, read out of `cx.active_drag` through
// `DragMoveEvent::drag` (`:70`) and, on the drop, handed in as the listener's own argument.
//
// # What the red looks like, and what it is
//
// A `src|drag` raised twice with nothing heard at all from the target — no `over`, no `out`, no
// `drop`, and no payload — suggests that the X server merged two motions into one, so that the
// move which *arms* the drag is also the move that enters the target, too late for a listener that
// runs on the capture pass (`elements/div.rs:282-296`) of the very event whose bubble pass arms
// the drag (`:2151-2175`).
//
// That reading is wrong, and a window-level `move` listener on the source is what says so: gpui is
// handed **eight** moves for the eight the probe posts, in order, with both motions of the first
// gesture among them. The moves arrive and the drag is armed; what does not happen is the report.
// `window::current()` is `0` inside a listener gpui calls after the frame that registered it —
// only the frame itself runs inside `window::with` (`gpui-binding/src/app.rs:51`) — so a reporter
// that reads its window there writes into the window that does not exist, and the probe, which is
// a frame, never sees it. Every other reporter in `view.rs` is handed a captured `win` by its
// caller for exactly this reason.
//
// # What is asserted, and against what
//
// Every number below is what `xdotool` was told, not what the binding says about itself: the
// pointer is posted to named points in the binding's own origin, the target's rectangle is known
// from its style string, and the count of each event is the count of *transitions* the gesture
// earned — one entry, one exit, one entry again, one drop.
//
// The last gesture is a **negative control**, and it is the half that says the rest is not a
// coincidence: a second drag from the same source, past the same threshold, released on empty
// window must raise a second `drag` and **no** second `drop`, `over` or `out`.
//
// Every failure is counted as well as printed, and the verdict reads the count, so nothing below
// can fail and then fall through to a passing line.

use gpui
use std::os::exec
use std::process
use std::strconv

/// The title the window is opened with, and what `xdotool` is told to look for. One word, so the
/// search cannot match anything else on the display.
const TITLE: String = "dd2drag"

const WIN_W: f64 = 700.0
const WIN_H: f64 = 500.0

/// The source: the element that is picked up, from its own style string below.
const SRC_X: f64 = 40.0
const SRC_Y: f64 = 40.0
const SRC_W: f64 = 140.0
const SRC_H: f64 = 140.0

/// The target: the element a drag lands on.
const TGT_X: f64 = 400.0
const TGT_Y: f64 = 60.0
const TGT_W: f64 = 220.0
const TGT_H: f64 = 220.0

/// The middle of each, which is where the pointer is posted.
const SRC_AT_X: f64 = SRC_X + SRC_W / 2.0
const SRC_AT_Y: f64 = SRC_Y + SRC_H / 2.0
const TGT_AT_X: f64 = TGT_X + TGT_W / 2.0
const TGT_AT_Y: f64 = TGT_Y + TGT_H / 2.0

/// A point on the way from one box to the other and inside neither: past the source's right edge
/// at 180 and short of the target's left edge at 400, so that **arming** the drag and **entering**
/// the target are two different moves and either can be read on its own. A single jump from the
/// source to the target would arm and enter on one event, and a binding that confused the two
/// would still pass.
const MID_X: f64 = 300.0
const MID_Y: f64 = 110.0

/// Below both boxes — the source ends at 180, the target at 280 — and well inside the 700 by 500
/// client area. Where a drag is released when nothing should be dropped.
const OFF_X: f64 = 250.0
const OFF_Y: f64 = 420.0

/// Run a program and answer its stdout, trimmed, with anything that went wrong printed.
fn shell(program: String, args: Vec<String>) -> String {
    match exec::run(program.clone(), args) {
        Ok(out) => {
            if out.code != 0 {
                println(f"FAIL DD2 {program} exited {out.code}: {out.stderr.trim()}")
            }
            out.stdout.trim().clone()
        }
        Err(problem) => {
            println(f"FAIL DD2 could not run {program}: {problem}")
            String::new()
        }
    }
}

/// One `key=value` line out of `xdotool getwindowgeometry --shell`, as a number, or `-1.0`.
fn field(text: String, key: String) -> f64 {
    for line in text.split("\n") {
        if let Some((name, value)) = line.split_once("=") {
            if name.trim() == key {
                return number(value.trim().clone())
            }
        }
    }
    -1.0
}

/// A number, or `-1.0` if the text was not one.
fn number(text: String) -> f64 {
    match strconv::parse_f64(text) {
        Ok(value) => value,
        Err(_) => -1.0,
    }
}

/// The xdotool words that post one pointer move. `--sync` makes the helper wait until the pointer
/// really is there, so the next thing the probe posts cannot overtake it.
fn move_to(x: f64, y: f64) -> Vec<String> {
    #["mousemove", "--sync", f"{x.round() as i64}", f"{y.round() as i64}"]
}

/// The xdotool words for one whole drag: a press where the pointer already is, a move to the
/// middle point, a move to `(x, y)`, and — for the gestures that end — a release.
///
/// **One invocation and not four**, so that nothing the probe does between frames can slip
/// between the press and the first move: gpui arms a drag on the *move* that follows a press, and
/// a press with no move after it in the same gesture is a click, not a drag.
fn drag_to(x: f64, y: f64, release: bool) -> Vec<String> {
    let mx = f"{MID_X.round() as i64}"
    let my = f"{MID_Y.round() as i64}"
    let tx = f"{x.round() as i64}"
    let ty = f"{y.round() as i64}"
    if release {
        #[
            "mousedown", "1", "mousemove", "--sync", mx.clone(), my.clone(),
            "mousemove", "--sync", tx.clone(), ty.clone(), "mouseup", "1",
        ]
    } else {
        #[
            "mousedown", "1", "mousemove", "--sync", mx.clone(), my.clone(),
            "mousemove", "--sync", tx.clone(), ty.clone(),
        ]
    }
}

/// A point as it will be compared: `"12.5,30.25"`, to two decimals.
fn point(x: f64, y: f64) -> String {
    f"{x:.2},{y:.2}"
}

/// Whether two points are the same to within a hundredth of a pixel.
fn same(x: f64, y: f64, ex: f64, ey: f64) -> bool {
    let dx = x - ex
    let dy = y - ey
    (if dx < 0.0 { -dx } else { dx }) < 0.01 && (if dy < 0.0 { -dy } else { dy }) < 0.01
}

/// Write a failure down: print it, and count it. The verdict at the end reads the count, which is
/// what stops any check here from failing and then falling through to a passing line.
fn fail(message: String) {
    println(f"FAIL DD2 {message}")
    gpui::set_int("fails", gpui::state_int("fails", 0) + 1)
}

/// How many of the recorded `"<id>|<kind>"` entries are named by `want`. Counted and not read from
/// the store, because the store holds one slot per key and this probe is about how many times each
/// of these arrived.
fn count(ids: Vec<String>, want: String) -> i64 {
    let mut n = 0
    for i in 0..ids.len() {
        if ids[i].clone() == want.clone() {
            n = n + 1
        }
    }
    n
}

/// One reading against what it should be, with the failure printed if they differ.
fn expect(what: String, got: i64, want: i64) {
    if got != want {
        fail(f"{what} read {got}, not {want}")
    }
}

/// The same for a flag.
fn expect_flag(what: String, got: bool, want: bool) {
    if got != want {
        fail(f"{what} read {got}, not {want}")
    }
}

/// And for a string.
fn expect_text(what: String, got: String, want: String) {
    if got != want {
        fail(f"{what} read `{got}`, not `{want}`")
    }
}

/// Where the pointer is posted, from the binding's own origin.
fn at(ox: f64, oy: f64, x: f64, y: f64) -> (f64, f64) {
    (ox + x, oy + y)
}

fn main() {
    let outcome = gpui::run(TITLE, WIN_W, WIN_H, || {
        let f = gpui::frame()
        gpui::animate()

        // Every event, on the frame it arrives on, into a list rather than read where it is
        // wanted: the store holds one slot per key, and a reading taken after a second `over`
        // cannot say anything about the first.
        for event in gpui::take_events() {
            let id, kind = gpui::event_parts(event.clone())
            gpui::list_push("ids", f"{id}|{kind}")
        }

        // ---- frame 3: find the window, and put the pointer on the source --------------------
        //
        // The origin is the binding's own, `gpui::window_field("inner_x")`, and not `xdotool`'s
        // reading of the same window: the binding's is the number a pointer event is measured
        // against, so it is the one to post relative to. They are compared rather than assumed
        // equal, because a window manager that frames the client puts the two apart.
        if f == 3 && gpui::state_int("phase", 0) == 0 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 1)
            let found = shell("xdotool", #["search", "--name", TITLE])
            let hits = found.lines()
            if hits.is_empty() {
                fail(f"no window named {TITLE} on this display (xdotool said `{found}`)")
                gpui::quit()
            } else {
                let geometry = shell(
                    "xdotool",
                    #["getwindowgeometry", "--shell", hits[0].trim().clone()],
                )
                let win_x = field(geometry.clone(), "X")
                let win_y = field(geometry.clone(), "Y")
                let ox = gpui::window_field("inner_x")
                let oy = gpui::window_field("inner_y")
                if !same(win_x, win_y, ox, oy) {
                    println(f"note DD2 xdotool puts the window at {point(win_x, win_y)} and the binding at {point(ox, oy)}; posting relative to the binding's")
                }
                gpui::set_float("origin_x", ox)
                gpui::set_float("origin_y", oy)
                let spot = at(ox, oy, SRC_AT_X, SRC_AT_Y)
                let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
            }
        }

        // ---- the one gesture, one step per frame ---------------------------------------------
        //
        // **One `xdotool` invocation per step, and one reading between each pair of steps.** A
        // step and a reading share a frame only because a reading has nothing to do until the
        // events it reads have arrived; every reading here is taken three frames after the step it
        // reads, and `gos` blocks that frame inside `exec::run` while the helper runs, so the
        // events are queued before the reading happens. Two moves are never posted inside one
        // invocation, because then the reading that belongs between them has nowhere to go.
        //
        // What the shape buys is not timing but **separability**: the press is one event, the move
        // that arms the drag is another, the move that enters the target is a third, and each is
        // read on its own. A drag posted as one motion from the source to the target would arm and
        // enter on the same event, and a binding that confused the two would still pass.
        //
        // A gesture posted as one invocation reads nothing at all from the target, and the
        // diagnosis that suggests itself is that X compresses motion events — a plausible story,
        // and wrong. A window-level `move` listener on the source, counting every move gpui is
        // handed, says **eight** for the eight the probe posts, in order, with the two motions of
        // the first gesture among them. The moves arrive. What does not is the report: the `over`
        // and `drop` listeners read their window from `window::current()`, which is `0` inside a
        // listener gpui calls after the frame that registered it — see the note on `report_drop`
        // in `gpui-binding/src/view.rs`. The instrument that said so is gone; the shape it left
        // behind is this one.
        let phase = gpui::state_int("phase", 0)
        if f == 6 && phase == 1 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 2)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", #["mousedown", "1"])
        }
        if f == 9 && phase == 2 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 3)
            let spot = at(gpui::state_float("origin_x", 0.0), gpui::state_float("origin_y", 0.0), MID_X, MID_Y)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
        }
        if f == 12 && phase == 3 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 4)
            let spot = at(gpui::state_float("origin_x", 0.0), gpui::state_float("origin_y", 0.0), TGT_AT_X, TGT_AT_Y)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
        }
        if f == 15 && phase == 4 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 5)
            expect("the `drag` the source raised", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "src|drag"), 1)
            expect_flag("the target's `over` while a payload is over it", gpui::state_bool("tgt.over", false), true)
            expect("the `over` the target raised", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|over"), 1)
            expect("the `drop` the target has raised — none, the button is still down", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|drop"), 0)
            expect_text("the payload the target was handed", gpui::state_text("tgt.drag_value", ""), "src")
        }
        if f == 18 && phase == 5 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 6)
            let spot = at(gpui::state_float("origin_x", 0.0), gpui::state_float("origin_y", 0.0), OFF_X, OFF_Y)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
        }
        if f == 21 && phase == 6 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 7)
            expect_flag("the target's `over` after the drag left it", gpui::state_bool("tgt.over", true), false)
            expect("the `out` the target raised", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|out"), 1)
            expect("the `drop` the target has raised — still none", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|drop"), 0)
        }
        if f == 24 && phase == 7 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 8)
            let spot = at(gpui::state_float("origin_x", 0.0), gpui::state_float("origin_y", 0.0), TGT_AT_X, TGT_AT_Y)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
        }
        if f == 27 && phase == 8 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 9)
            expect_flag("the target's `over` when the drag came back", gpui::state_bool("tgt.over", false), true)
            expect("the `over` count after a second entry", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|over"), 2)
            expect("the `out` count — the drag has left once", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|out"), 1)
        }
        if f == 30 && phase == 9 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 10)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", #["mouseup", "1"])
        }
        if f == 33 && phase == 10 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 11)
            expect("the `drop` the target raised", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|drop"), 1)
            expect_flag("the target's `over` after the payload landed", gpui::state_bool("tgt.over", true), false)
            expect_text("the payload the drop carried", gpui::state_text("tgt.drag_value", ""), "src")
            let tx = gpui::state_float("tgt.x", -99.0)
            let ty = gpui::state_float("tgt.y", -99.0)
            let ex = TGT_AT_X - TGT_X
            let ey = TGT_AT_Y - TGT_Y
            if !same(tx, ty, ex, ey) {
                fail(f"the drop was reported at {point(tx, ty)} in the target's own space, not the {point(ex, ey)} it was posted at")
            }
        }

        // ---- and the negative control: the same source, released on empty window ------------
        if f == 36 && phase == 11 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 12)
            let spot = at(gpui::state_float("origin_x", 0.0), gpui::state_float("origin_y", 0.0), SRC_AT_X, SRC_AT_Y)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
        }
        if f == 39 && phase == 12 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 13)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", #["mousedown", "1"])
        }
        if f == 42 && phase == 13 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 14)
            let spot = at(gpui::state_float("origin_x", 0.0), gpui::state_float("origin_y", 0.0), MID_X, MID_Y)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
        }
        if f == 45 && phase == 14 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 15)
            let spot = at(gpui::state_float("origin_x", 0.0), gpui::state_float("origin_y", 0.0), OFF_X, OFF_Y)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", move_to(spot.0, spot.1))
        }
        if f == 48 && phase == 15 {
            gpui::set_int("phase", 16)
            let _ = shell("xdotool", #["mouseup", "1"])
        }
        if f == 52 && phase == 16 {
            expect("the `drag` count after a second gesture", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "src|drag"), 2)
            expect("the `drop` count — the second drag ended on empty window", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|drop"), 1)
            expect("the `over` count — the second drag never crossed the target", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|over"), 2)
            expect("the `out` count", count(gpui::list_all("ids"), "tgt|out"), 1)

            if gpui::state_int("fails", 0) == 0 {
                println(f"OK DD2 gpui's own drag protocol reached the program: a press on the source that moved past `DRAG_THRESHOLD` armed a drag and raised `drag`; a target heard `over` while the payload was over it and `out` when it left, each once per crossing; the release over it raised `drop`, carrying the source's own payload in `<id>.drag_value` and the point in the target's own space; and a second drag released on empty window raised a second `drag` and no second `drop`")
            }
            gpui::quit()
        }

        // The source and the target, and nothing else in the window that a pointer could reach.
        let src = gpui::on_mouse(
            gpui::rect(f"absolute left={SRC_X} top={SRC_Y} w={SRC_W} h={SRC_H} bg=#ff8800"),
            "drag",
            "src",
        )
        let tgt = gpui::on_mouse(
            gpui::rect(f"absolute left={TGT_X} top={TGT_Y} w={TGT_W} h={TGT_H} bg=#2f6fd0"),
            "over drop",
            "tgt",
        )
        let root = gpui::children(gpui::stack("size=full bg=#ffffff"), #[src, tgt])
        gpui::root(root)
    })

    if let Err(problem) = outcome {
        eprintln(f"gpui::run stopped with an error: {problem}")
        process::exit(1)
    }
}
